The file looks like this:

{code:json}{
  "entities": [
    {
      "name": ""
    },
    {
      "name": "Augnet"
    },
...
  ]
}{code}

The entity names are taken from `router.entity_name` for all the encountered routers.

I attempted to make this work with partial generation:

* when partial, read the existing entity list, add any new entities seen when looking at the current routers
* when full ({{generate:api all}}), just generate everything from scratch

It turns out though that there’s a new EntityListBuilder created for each router, and therefore this class is stateless.

The state is kept in the FileManager, in a blocked file, which keeps chunks of the file to be written.



The better way to do this would be to have an EntityListFileManager, that can:

* read the current state (JSON.parse)
* keep entities in a Set<string> and be able to add new entities
* finally write the state (JSON.stringify)



This needs a lot of changes though, because it goes against the apigen philosophy of “stateless” components per service.

*Since there’s a comment in Builders.ts saying:* *_“//for information purposes only, can stay, as changes very often”_**, I choose not to invest time in this.*

I’ve created two tables - {{a}} and {{b}} - with the following structure:

{code:sql}create table b(
    `id` BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY ,
    `domain` ENUM ('supply', 'rides_and_rental', 'delivery', 'cars', 'providers') NOT NULL, 
    `group_id` BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL
);{code}

For table {{a}} the indexed columns go from low cardinality to high:

{code:sql}ALTER TABLE a 
ADD INDEX idx(domain, group_id, id);{code}

For table {{b}} columns go from high cardinality to low:

{code:sql}ALTER TABLE b 
ADD INDEX idx(id, group_id, domain);{code}

The {{group_id}} column should have lower cardinality than {{id}} but higher than {{domain}}

